Here is the full **testsmells.org 19-smell academic catalog** audit of `OrderProcessorTests.cs`.

---

## Summary Dashboard

| Severity | Smell Count | Affected Tests |
|----------|-------------|----------------|
| High     | 4           | 4              |
| Medium   | 5           | 5              |
| Low      | 1           | all (class)    |
| **Total**| **10**      | **8 of 9 methods** |

---

## Findings by Severity

### ⛔ HIGH

---

#### 1 · Conditional Test Logic

**File:** `OrderProcessorTests.cs` · **Method:** `ProcessOrder_SetsCorrectStatus` (line 24)

```csharp
if (result.TotalAmount > 100)
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
else
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
```

Only one branch ever runs, so the test never verifies the other. This is a hidden gap.

**Fix — split into two explicit tests:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_StandardAmount_SetsStandardStatus()
{
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-CHEAP", 1) } };
    var result = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory).ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.AreEqual("StandardProcessed", result.Status);
}

[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_PremiumAmount_SetsPremiumStatus()
{
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-EXPENSIVE", 1) } };
    var result = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory).ProcessOrder(order);
    Assert.AreEqual("PremiumProcessed", result.Status);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** One status path is permanently untested; regressions in that path will never be caught.

---

#### 2 · Assertion-Free Test (a.k.a. Unknown Test)

**File:** `OrderProcessorTests.cs` · **Method:** `ProcessOrder_CompletesWithoutError` (line 43)

```csharp
processor.ProcessOrder(order);
// ← no assertion, no expected-exception attribute
```

The test passes as long as no exception is thrown — it verifies nothing about the result.

**Fix:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public void ProcessOrder_ValidOrder_ReturnsCompletedResult()
{
    var processor = new OrderProcessor(_db, _email, _inventory);
    var order = new Order { Items = { new OrderItem("SKU-1", 1) } };

    var result = processor.ProcessOrder(order);

    Assert.AreEqual("Completed", result.Status);
    Assert.IsTrue(result.TotalAmount > 0);
}
```

**Risk if unfixed:** False confidence — coverage increases but bugs pass silently.

---

#### 3 · Sleepy Test

**File:** `OrderProcessorTests.cs` · **Method:** `ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent` (line 91)

```csharp
Thread.Sleep(2000);
```

Hardcoded 2-second sleep makes the suite slow and flaky; on a loaded CI machine the delay may not be enough, and on a fast machine it wastes time.

**Fix — use a polling/wait helper or `async`/`await`:**

```csharp
[TestMethod]
public async Task ProcessOrder_AsyncNotification_IsSent()
{
